<!--
    OstrichChallenge - Sandbox Option Presets

    The hotel's difficulty tiers, in their OWN group in the Sandbox Options preset dropdown
    rather than the shared "Modded" group. Loaded by the mod itself (see Presets.cs), because
    sandbox_overrides.xml always hands presets to the "Modded" group whatever their category says.

    THE CODES BELOW ARE THE HOSPITAL CHALLENGE'S LADDER, USED AS A STARTING POINT.
    They are settings values only, and they were tuned for a different challenge: a hospital, on
    foot, with a gyrocopter at the end. They give a working group from the first load instead of
    six empty tiers, and they should be regenerated for the hotel.

    TO FILL IN A TIER PROPERLY
      1. In game: New Game, Sandbox Options, set everything the way you want it.
      2. Use the copy button beside the sandbox code box.
      3. Paste it into code="" below, replacing what is there.
      4. Save and restart the game. No rebuild needed.

    A preset whose code is FILLMEIN is skipped at load, so a half-finished tier never appears
    broken in the menu.

    WORTH CHECKING when regenerating, because this mod's own systems read them:
      LootRespawnDays   NOTE, CHANGED IN v1.7.0: the mod forces this to 0 and it no longer has
                        anything to do with containers. It drives the SLEEPER volume respawn,
                        and anything above zero refills cleared rooms with zombies. Leave it.
                        Container restocking is the Room Restock option above.
      XPMultiplier      how fast the player levels, which decides how soon they can fight him
      EnemySpawnMode    wandering hordes are the ground floor's pressure; off makes it a museum
      BloodMoonFrequency  check-in is suspended on horde nights, so this changes the rhythm
      StarterSkillPoints  EVERY tier below currently sets this to None against a vanilla default
                        of 4, which is why the first play-through began with no points at all.
                        Whatever a tier should give, set it deliberately rather than leaving it.
      ZombieMoveNight / ZombieFeralMove / ZombieBMMove
                        the stalker's speed once he has the player. GetMoveSpeedAggro indexes
                        [0.00 Walk, 0.35 Jog, 0.70 Run, 1.00 Sprint, 1.35 Nightmare], and there is
                        no separate setting for him: he moves at whatever every other zombie does.
                        Daytime is vanilla's own default (Walk) unless a tier overrides it.

    ROOM RESTOCK (v1.7.0) — AND HOW TO ADD IT TO A CODE WITHOUT REGENERATING ONE

    The mod now registers its own sandbox option, "Room Restock", in the Resources tab, with
    four values: Never / On check-in / Daily / Loot Respawn Days. It behaves like any vanilla
    option, so setting it in the options screen and hitting the copy button captures it.

    It can also be added to an EXISTING code by hand, because the code format is keyed rather
    than positional. After the leading version character the code is a run of three-character
    triplets: two characters naming the option, one naming the chosen value. Unknown options are
    skipped on load, and the order of the triplets does not matter.

    Our option is id 600, which encodes as "XC", and the values are A, B, C, D in the order
    above. So APPEND one of these to the end of any code:

        XCA   Never          nothing in the building ever restocks
        XCB   On check-in    a room restocks the morning after a guest checks into it
        XCC   Daily          an emptied container restocks a day later, anywhere
        XCD   Loot Respawn Days   the vanilla slider decides, as it did before v1.7.0

    Nothing else in the code changes and no other setting shifts, because nothing is positional.
    A code carrying XC loaded WITHOUT this mod simply skips it.

    THE INTENDED LADDER, which is why the option exists:
      Night Porter    XCC  Daily      the soft tier; the hotel keeps feeding you
      Duty Manager    XCB  On check-in
      Full House      XCB  On check-in
      No Vacancies    XCB  On check-in
      Do Not Disturb  XCB  On check-in
      The Last Guest  XCA  Never      what you did not take on the way up is gone

    A tier that says nothing gets On check-in, which is the option's default.

    ATTRIBUTES
      name              required, internal id, no spaces
      code              required, the sandbox code from the options screen
      localized_name    what players see, a Localization.csv key
      description_key   the blurb, a Localization.csv key
      difficulty_rating shows a skull and xN; keep ascending
      default           "true" marks the default selection for a new game
      icon              optional; @modfolder(OstrichChallenge):Resources/file.png also works

    THE TIER ART (v1.5.3)

    Six pulp-horror covers, one per tier, in Resources/. No DLL change and no XUi change was
    needed: Presets.cs hands the whole XElement to the game's own LoadPresetFromXml, so the
    game parses icon= itself, SandboxPresetInfo has a native Icon field, and the menu draws it
    with an XUi <texture> that runs the path through the standard mod-path resolver and loads
    it off disk. No asset bundle. This was already documented in the Area 7 and Hospital preset
    files and should have been read before it was investigated.

    A BARE SPRITE NAME RENDERS NOTHING. icon="ui_game_symbol_*" does not work. Only a vanilla
    resources path (Data/Sandbox/icons/user_custom) or a @modfolder path to a real file.

    THE EXTENSION IS IGNORED AND THE FORMAT IS SNIFFED. These six are JPEG data with .png
    names: 627 KB for the set instead of several megabytes as PNGs. Hospital's equivalent six
    came to 2.1 MB the same way.

    DRAWN AT 180x180, ASPECT KEPT, so square is the safe shape. These are 512x512, which leaves
    headroom without carrying a 1024 into a 180px slot.

    THE THUMBNAIL TEST, learned on Hospital: Walk-In and Flatline shipped with border colours
    three points apart on every channel, so the easiest and hardest tiers were indistinguishable
    as thumbnails and both had to be redrawn. These six were measured onto a deliberate ramp
    instead, all at lightness 41% and saturation 62% so they carry equal weight, with only the
    hue moving:

        Night Porter   #286EAA  208 deg      No Vacancies    #AA5A28   23 deg
        Duty Manager   #28AA64  148 deg      Do Not Disturb  #AA2832  355 deg
        Full House     #AA8C28   46 deg      The Last Guest  #000000  none

    The Last Guest leaves the hue wheel entirely, which is the point: it is the only tier that
    is not a colour. If its frame disappears against a dark menu background, #1A1A1A gives it
    an edge while still reading as the black one.
-->
